Idle mode that can be switched while apps run (the kext's boost policy). NullMoth's own change (not an upstream Mesa
commit).
NVK asks the GPU for full clocks with a 3 s lease that a busy process renews at most once a second
(nvk-perf-boost-burst.patch). Its shape came from NVRM_610_BOOST, read once when the process opened the device, so
NVMetrics' idle mode (NVRM_610_BOOST=0 in nvrm610.conf) reached only the apps started after it was switched, and
switching it off left the running ones without boost until they were restarted. NVRM.kext now holds a policy of three
values (kexts/NVRM/nvrm_boost_policy_abi.h; nvkmd/nvrm/nvrm_boost_policy_abi.h here is a byte-identical copy):
undecided (since boot: each process follows NVRM_610_BOOST as before), idle mode, and normal. A root process sets it
through user-client selector 13; NVK reads it through the same selector each time it would renew. In idle mode a
process asks for nothing and clears the lease or hold it still has; in normal it boosts per burst (or holds, with
NVRM_610_BOOST=hold) even if it started with NVRM_610_BOOST=0. A kext without the selector is asked once and the
process keeps NVRM_610_BOOST, as in 3.0. Other RM clients' boosts (NVMetrics' own, nvmtl-boost) are not touched.
Also:
- NVRM_610_BOOST is read under its full name in nvrm610.conf too (the last such line wins), then as 2.9's short
  "BOOST=" line, at each device creation and without printing. nvrm610_value only ever matched the short form in the
  file, so a Vulkan app without the Metal plugin (which copies the file into the environment) did not see the line
  NVMetrics writes.
- A refused lease or hold is asked again 30 s later instead of never again in that process: a refusal while the GPU
  came back from sleep turned boost off until the app was restarted.
- A hold process also looks at the policy at most once a second while it submits, so idle mode clears its hold at
  its next submission. One that submits nothing keeps its hold until it submits again or exits.
- A hold is cleared as it was asked, with the CUDA bit, and then without it as before: the RM's internal interface
  clears the two kinds of boost apart, and which clear the firmware honours for a hold is not in the open sources.
- One renewal runs at a time (boostBusy). The deadline alone let a second thread renew while the first one's RM
  call waited at the kext's power gate through a standby, and the two then raced on what the process holds.

